cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite the versioned release of this preprint. It is not peer reviewed, and no DOI is assigned until an archived release mints one. AI disclosure: AI systems performed most source retrieval, drafting, quantitative reconstruction and release engineering under direction of the accountable author. Every quantitative claim is recomputed from the archived records by `analysis/reproduce.py`; every source carries a reading grade recording how much of it was actually read. Readers should weigh claims by those grades. Codex 6.1: Codex 6.1 is an AI system. It is credited for the research and drafting work it performed at the request of the accountable author. It cannot hold scholarly, legal or ethical accountability for the work; the human author does. Claude (Anthropic): Claude is an AI system. It assembled the integrated edition, wrote the introduction and conclusion from the parts' findings, and built the reproduction, metadata, ontology, provenance and release pipeline. Accountable author: Morgan Sennhauser."
title: "White Repopulation: Reproduction without Preservation"
version: 0.2.0
abstract: "Settler institutions in the United States have described their future as the increase of a white population. That description joins four histories that move independently: the births and survival of people counted as white; the changing rules for who counts; eligibility to found a preferred future population; and the reproduction of property, jurisdiction and labor relations. This study separates them and asks which one institutions preserved when they conflicted. Four documentary and quantitative strands answer in turn. Franklin's population arithmetic, race-suicide pronatalism and Virginia's racial-integrity law show a purity ideal that bent when its rigid application threatened elite genealogies. A reconstruction of births, 1790–1859, finds that roughly one U.S. birth in six (17.20% in 1800–1849) was a birth into hereditary slavery, a reproduction of property that conferred no membership. Antebellum property law divided a child's status, title and anticipated issue across time. Transatlantic sending, receiving and policy-borrowing cases show settler growth drawing on selected, indebted and assisted migrants through case-specific institutions rather than one coordinated design. Across the strands, institutions preserved the authority to classify and to appropriate reproduction more reliably than the reproductive opportunities of every person classified white. The stronger proposition, that a deferred ideal can exhaust the population it claims to protect, remains a conditional hypothesis; the paper states the cohort and institutional evidence that would test it."
