  notes: "Subtitle: The future white, captive births, and the transatlantic reproduction of settler rule. Not peer reviewed. An anonymous contributor acknowledged for research direction. No DOI assigned. CARE authority-to-control: No Indigenous data are collected. Where the paper discusses Indigenous political continuity (for example Pamunkey opposition to Virginia's 1930 law), it relies on published scholarship and public records, and treats colonial administrative recognition as distinct from Indigenous self-determination. Requests from a named nation concerning those passages are handled under docs/data-governance.md. CARE responsibility: Source records state what each document can and cannot show, including the bias of court and enslaver records that seldom preserve the voices of enslaved mothers and children. CARE ethics: Persons named in historical records are not reduced to counts; the paper does not reproduce identifying detail beyond what its published sources already make public. CARE collective-benefit: The study concerns people whose reproduction was appropriated by settler institutions, and Indigenous nations whose recognition those institutions conditioned. Findings are released openly so descendant communities and their scholars can use, contest and correct them without cost."
